TITLE:: RLLine
summary:: A line over chords: a rhythm and pitches drawn note by note from what a note knows
categories:: Libraries>reLation
related:: Classes/RLRecall, Classes/RLFit, Classes/RLWalker, Guides/reLation

DESCRIPTION::
The reverse of a reading: where link::Classes/RLFit:: prices the chords a piece has, RLLine draws notes over chords it is given.

A note's pitch is drawn from a product of small counted tables ("experts"), each saying how likely a pitch is from one point of view, each raised to a weight:

table::
## code::\three:: (or code::\two::, code::\step::) || the step from the note before, given the two steps before that (arpeggios, scales, turns)
## code::\chord:: || the tone above the chord's root, by the chord's kind, on or off the beat
## code::\key:: || the tone above the tune's tonic, by its mode
## code::\place:: || the pitch against the line's median (the register)
## code::\motive:: || not counted on other solos: what this line has done so far after the same last steps (link::Classes/RLRecall::): a figure that comes back
## code::\stay:: || whether the note repeats the pitch before, by how many times that pitch has been said already
::

A note's start is drawn the same way: the gaps before it (code::\two::, code::\last::, code::\gap::), where in the beat it lands (code::\lands::), the tempo (code::\tempo::); then how long it sounds within its gap.

The tables are counted on solos by HomewareSC's code::Math/Category_Theory/fit/jazz_know.py:: and code::jazz_rhythm.py::, which write the two models this class reads. A table is kept there on one of two grounds: solos not counted cost fewer bits with it, or lines drawn with it are nearer the players' in what they are made of (code::jazz_shape.py::: how often a line repeats a pitch, moves by step or by leap, turns, comes back to its own figures). Counted on bebop, hard bop, cool and swing solos of the Weimar Jazz Database, a note's pitch costs 2.98 bits on bebop solos not counted and 2.87 on Charlie Parker's, never counted (5.93 knowing nothing); lines drawn from it come back to their own figures as often as his do.

A model may say other weights for drawing than those that tell real lines best (code::\draw::): the latter flatten each table, and a line drawn with them may stick on a pitch.

Pitches are MIDI notes and pitch classes those of twelve steps; time is in beats. Not modelled: the phrase (when a line breathes, how long it runs: three counts of it gave nothing), dynamics, articulation beyond how long a note sounds.

code::
~line = RLLine.read(pitchModelPath, rhythmModelPath);
// twelve bars of a blues in F
~chords = [5, 10, 5, 5, 10, 10, 5, 5, 0, 10, 5, 0].collect { |r, i| (onset: i * 4, dur: 4, root: r, pcs: [r, r + 4, r + 7, r + 10] % 12) };
~notes = ~line.draw(~chords, 0, 48, key: [5, \major], median: 65, lowest: 53, highest: 79, tempo: 180, seed: 4);
~notes.collect(_[\midinote]);
RLLine.pbind(~notes, 48).play(TempoClock(3));

// in a piece (reCurrent): the line as a beat
~beat = ~song.layer(\core).addBeat(\line, [instrument: \default, dur_flex: RLLine.deltas(~notes, 48), midinote: Pseq(~notes.collect(_[\midinote]), inf)]);

// pitches for a rhythm of one's own; a rhythm alone
~line.pitches([[0, 0.5], [0.5, 0.5], [1, 1], [2, 0.5]], ~chords, [5, \major], first: 65);
~line.rhythm(0, 8, 180);
::

CLASSMETHODS::

METHOD:: new
argument:: pitchModel
A model of the pitches, as read (link::#*readModel::). An expert it does not know is an Error.
argument:: rhythmModel
A model of the rhythm, or nil (then link::#-rhythm:: and link::#-draw:: are Errors, and link::#-pitches:: takes a rhythm of one's own).

METHOD:: read
From the two files.

METHOD:: readModel
One model from its file.

METHOD:: gapKind
A gap in twelfths of a beat as the tables see what came before: the common ones each a kind of their own, the others by size.

METHOD:: chordKind
A chord's kind from its tones above its root: 0 major, 1 dominant (a major third and a minor seventh), 2 minor, 3 diminished.

METHOD:: deltas
From a line's notes, the time from each note to the next (the last to code::end::): a beat's durations, a Pbind's code::\dur::.

METHOD:: pbind
The line as a Pbind: code::\midinote::, code::\dur:: (the deltas), code::\sustain:: (how long each note sounds).

METHOD:: common
The gaps that are kinds of their own, in twelfths of a beat.

INSTANCEMETHODS::

METHOD:: draw
A line over chords from code::start:: to code::end::, in beats: a rhythm, then pitches on it. Events with code::\onset::, code::\dur:: (how long the note sounds) and code::\midinote::.
argument:: chords
Events with code::\onset::, code::\dur::, code::\root:: and code::\pcs:: (pitch classes of twelve steps), in order.
argument:: start
argument:: end
argument:: key
code::[tonic pitch class, \major or \minor]::, or nil (the key then says nothing).
argument:: first
The first pitch; drawn like the others when nil.
argument:: median
The pitch the line stays about (the middle of its range when nil).
argument:: lowest
argument:: highest
Its range (the model's when nil).
argument:: tempo
In beats a minute; nil: what the tempo says of the rhythm is left out.
argument:: seed
The same seed gives the same line.
argument:: heat
Under 1 the likelier choices are taken more often. Lines drawn at 1 are the nearest to the players'.

METHOD:: rhythm
A rhythm alone: code::[[onset, how long it sounds], ...]::. The first note is at code::start::; a gap that would pass code::end:: ends it.

METHOD:: pitches
Pitches for a rhythm (code::[[onset, how long it sounds], ...]::): the MIDI notes. A rest of a beat or more starts a new phrase.

METHOD:: chances
The chances of every pitch (link::#-low:: to link::#-high::) for one note, given what it knows: an Event with code::\prev::, code::\before::, code::\before2:: (the pitches before it in its phrase), code::\afterRest::, code::\root:: and code::\kind:: (its chord), code::\beat::, code::\tonic:: and code::\minor::, code::\median::, code::\same:: (how many times the pitch before had been said already), code::\recalled:: (an link::Classes/RLRecall::'s chances).

METHOD:: pitchExperts
METHOD:: rhythmExperts
The experts of each model.

METHOD:: pitchModel
METHOD:: rhythmModel
METHOD:: low
METHOD:: high
The model's range of pitches.
